{"Cache key collision between userPermCacheKey and userPermDenialCacheKey - both use the same prefix pattern and same cache backend, so denial cache keys could shadow permission cache keys if strings happen to match, especially when name or parent are empty strings": 0.30171433091163635, "Denial cache check fires before permission cache check and DB fallback, so a user denied before a resource is created will be blocked for up to 30 seconds even after permission is granted, contradicting the PR's stated goal": 0.776441216468811, "No test verifying that when a user is later granted permission, the denial cache does NOT permanently block access - the test suite has no case that sets a denial cache entry, grants the user permission in the DB, then calls Check and verifies the user is now allowed": 0.571278989315033, "No code path that ever deletes or invalidates a denial cache entry when a permission is granted - permDenialCache only writes when !allowed": 0.8059882521629333, "getCachedIdentityPermissions calls GetUserIdentifiers before checking permission cache, so the fast path cache check is not actually free - it always incurs at least an idCache lookup and potentially a DB query": 0.34933915734291077, "If GetUserIdentifiers fails, getCachedIdentityPermissions returns an error causing the caller to fall through to getIdentityPermissions which calls GetUserIdentifiers again, doubling DB load on error paths": 0.3800242841243744, "NoopCache disables client-side cache but server-side permDenialCache still caches denials for 30 seconds, so users checking access before resource creation remain denied even after creating the resource": 0.783403217792511}